  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="Tag325" contextRef="C1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;A. General&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Note 1: Regulation and Operations&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Southwest Gas Transmission Company, a Limited Partnership (the Company) is subject to rate regulation by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC). Its sole customer is Southwest Gas Corporation (SWG), which is also its general partner, and which holds a 99% ownership interest in the Company. The other 1% interest is owned by a subsidiary that is wholly owned by SWG.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;See Note 4 below. SWG is a wholly owned subsidiary of Southwest Gas Holdings, Inc. The Company&amp;#8217;s system ends at the southern most receipt point of the Southwest Gas Corporation southern Nevada mainline located in the middle of the Colorado River approximately three miles north of the common boundary of Arizona, Nevada, and California. Accounting records are maintained in accordance with the FERC Uniform System of Accounts (USoA).&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;B. Accounting Policies and Updates&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Note 2: Basis of Reporting &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;The financial statements are presented in accordance with the reporting requirements of the FERC. This is a comprehensive basis of accounting substantially consistent with GAAP. Some variability can occur due to reporting requirements of the FERC form that distinguishes line items by the FERC USofA rather than by significant functional category as under U.S. GAAP. While the Company is a partnership, which is not a tax-paying entity (unlike its general partner), its share of the consolidated tax of its general partner is reflected herein. The general partner also owns a 100% interest in the limited partner and files a single consolidated tax return, which includes activity of the Company and the limited partner.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;C. Balance Sheet&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Note 3: Gas Plant&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Gas Plant is stated at original cost and includes an allowance for funds used during construction.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;SWG administers pension and other benefit plans such that when SWG employees work on Company-related construction projects, their labor and overhead costs (including benefit costs) are applied to Company work orders. The Company capitalizes eligible benefit costs when SWG employees work on Company-related construction projects.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 10.0pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Construction Work In Progress total of $1,162,579 is for the El Paso Natural Gas Company's Kinder Morgan supply lines of two meters with new Ultrasonic meters. The estimated completion date for the project is June 30, 2026.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Note 4: Proprietary Capital&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;The Company is organized as a limited partnership.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;As indicated above, SWG, the general partner, holds a 99% investment interest.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;Utility Financial Corp., a limited partner and a subsidiary of SWG, holds a 1% investment interest.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;The balance reported represents total partners&amp;#8217; capital.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;The retained earnings caption is therefore not applicable, but most closely defines these balances.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Related Party Transactions&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;As noted previously, the Company is a subsidiary of SWG. The Company provides transmission service exclusively to SWG. Amounts receivable from SWG are recorded in an intercompany receivable account.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Certain costs incurred by SWG associated with Company operations, such as income taxes related to Company earnings, in addition to plant-related and other expenditures (net of any general partner CIAC), are recorded in an intercompany payable account.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;In accordance with a letter agreement dated April 28, 2022, SWG agreed to make a Contribution in Aid of Construction (CIAC) to the Company to cover the actual cost of construction of the Low Flow Meter Run at the Company&amp;#8217;s interconnect, subject to the FERC approval of the proposed CIAC provisions proposed to be included in the Company&amp;#8217;s tariff. These provisions were approved in association with the settled rate case, and a CIAC in the amount of $1,529,056, in addition to $183,487 of federal tax gross-up, was recorded in the Company&amp;#8217;s records in June 2024.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;CIAC amortization on the federal tax gross-up amount of $183,487 has commenced, $12,233 was amortized in May 2025 and will be amortized during a 15-year period annually every May.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;In 2022-2023, SGTC constructed low flow meter run facilities at its interconnect with Transwestern Pipeline.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;This construction project was at the request of its Shipper and general partner, Southwest Gas Corporation.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;The Shipper agreed in a letter agreement to reimburse SGTC through a Contribution in Aid of Construction for the actual cost of construction.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;To accept the CIAC from the Shipper, SGTC needed to make a change to its FERC approved tariff.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;On April 26, 2024, the FERC approved the necessary tariff change as part of SGTC&amp;#8217;s last rate case, RP24-520.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;&lt;strong style="mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;"&gt;D. Statement of Income &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Note 5:&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;Revenue and Income&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;The rates of the Company are governed by its tariff and include an Annual Charge Adjustment (ACA) designed to cover the Company&amp;#8217;s share of the cost to administer the FERC natural gas regulatory program.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;The ACA rate, $0.0015 per dekatherm throughout the year is assessed each month to customers for each dekatherm of gas transported by the Company. While revenues overall are based on FERC-approved design, expenses include more variability. Operation expenses include costs of operating the pipeline, pipeline safety and outside services expense, in addition to other general costs reasonably allocable to the Company from the general partner.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;On March 11, 2024, the Company filed with the FERC a settlement agreement between the Company, SWG (its shipper), the Public Utilities Commission of Nevada, and the Nevada Bureau of Consumer Protection.&lt;font style="mso-spacerun: yes;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;As part of the settlement, the Company is expected to recover $274,464 from in-line-inspection related costs over a five-year period ($54,893 per year). On April 26, 2024, the FERC approved the settlement, which became effective May 1, 2024.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;Note: 6 Income Taxes&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="Normal"&gt;&lt;font style="font-size: 16pt; font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;"&gt;The relative split between current and deferred taxes is due to a variety of factors including true-ups of prior year tax returns. Tax benefits overall are reflective of losses where relevant. For 2025 the federal deferred income taxes of $21,117 includes a credit of $5,580,&amp;#160;for 2024 the ($324,101) includes a credit of $411,172. Components of income tax expense (benefit) shown in the Statements of Income are as follows:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table style="border-collapse: collapse; width: 22.5221%; height: 188px;" border="0"&gt;

&lt;tr style="height: 20px;"&gt;
&lt;td style="width: 16.8402%; height: 20px;"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/td&gt;
